course: dictionary
lesson: 14476
title: Reading food labels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Food labels often use words you may not know, but the other words on the label give you clues. «The milk in the fridge is pasteurised.» The label is about how the milk was made, so pasteurised tells you something about how safe it is to drink.
explain: Pasteurised means heated for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooled. Look for the clue: the food has been treated to make it safer. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The milk in the fridge is heated to kill germs, then cooled» still makes sense, so you know you are right.
explain: Pasteurised can also be a verb form, the past of pasteurise. «Yesterday the dairy pasteurised the milk before the trucks came.» Here it tells you that someone did the heating in the past. You can swap in a simple word, such as treated, to check.
fact: Pasteurised describes food that has been heated for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooled.
fact: «Pasteurised milk» is a common label you may see on bottles and cartons in shops.
fact: The opposite of pasteurised is unpasteurised, or raw, which means it has not been treated this way.
fact: The word comes from the name of a scientist, Louis Pasteur.
fact: In the US, the same word is spelled pasteurized, with a z.
exercise: «The milk in the fridge is pasteurised.» What does pasteurised mean? | Made from cows that live on a farm | Frozen solid so it lasts for years | *Heated for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooled | Mixed with sugar and fruit
exercise: The label says «made with pasteurised eggs». What does this tell the buyer? | The eggs are very old | *The eggs were treated to make them safer from germs | The eggs are bigger than normal | The eggs were found on a beach
exercise: Which word means the opposite of pasteurised? | Frozen | *Raw | Heated | Sweet
test: «Yesterday the farm pasteurised the juice.» What form of the verb is pasteurised here? | The name of a place | *The past tense of pasteurise | A type of fruit | A colour
test: Why do science lessons talk about pasteurised food? | *It is safer from germs like salmonella and listeria | It has more sugar in it | It is rare and hard to find | It is cheaper to buy
exam: Which best explains what pasteurised means? | *Heated for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooled | Cooked for hours until it turns brown | Stored in a freezer for a whole year | Coloured with a safe dye
